  {
    "id": "pw-phy-c11-misl-010",
    "subject": "Physics",
    "chapter": "Motion in a Straight Line",
    "topic": "Distance in nth Second",
    "questionNumber": 10,
    "statement": "A body starts from rest with uniform acceleration. What is the ratio of the distance travelled by the body during the 4th and 3rd second?",
    "statementImages": [],
    "options": [
      {
        "optionIndex": 1,
        "label": "A",
        "text": "7/5",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "markedPercentage": "64.44%"
      },
      {
        "optionIndex": 2,
        "label": "B",
        "text": "5/7",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "markedPercentage": "18.08%"
      },
      {
        "optionIndex": 3,
        "label": "C",
        "text": "7/3",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "markedPercentage": "12.22%"
      },
      {
        "optionIndex": 4,
        "label": "D",
        "text": "3/7",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "markedPercentage": "5.52%"
      }
    ],
    "correctOption": 1,
    "correctAnswer": "7/5",
    "accuracy": "64.27% of students got it right",
    "difficulty": "Easy",
    "solutionText": "For a body starting from rest (u = 0) with uniform acceleration a, distance travelled in the nth second is:\ns_n = u + (a/2)(2n - 1) = (a/2)(2n - 1)\nDistance during 4th second: s_4 = (a/2)(2*4 - 1) = 7a/2\nDistance during 3rd second: s_3 = (a/2)(2*3 - 1) = 5a/2\nRatio s_4 / s_3 = (7a/2) / (5a/2) = 7/5.\nCorrect answer: A (7/5)."
  },
